+    # The mirror of the two rules above, as M32 mirrored M31: refused, and the
+    # exclusion the lookup found is not among the citations. The stored answer
+    # to `breach-of-law` ("injured while being arrested for shoplifting") is
+    # not_covered citing 4.3, hazardous sports: the verdict is right and the
+    # person is shown the wrong clause to read.
+    #
+    # Only a refusal that rests on OTHER EXCLUSIONS alone is asked, so the
+    # question is which exclusion. That boundary was measured, not assumed.
+    # Asked about any refusal, with this same two-way wording, the model gave
+    # up a right clause for the looked-up one, 2 samples of 2 each: typhoid
+    # three weeks in, rightly refused under the 30-day wait, came back as
+    # "convicted of trespassing some years ago ... therefore the claim for
+    # treatment of typhoid is not covered"; Ayurvedic treatment at an
+    # unaccredited clinic lost its AYUSH clause the same way. A refusal under
+    # an exclusion that names the treatment (a nose job, bungee jumping, IVF,
+    # each beside an old arrest or conviction) kept its clause, 2 of 2 each.
+    # A clause with a waiting-period check is never "another exclusion":
+    # HDFC's specified-disease waiting period is typed `exclusion`.
+    #
+    # The re-ask does not carry the previous answer, so the line says what it
+    # was. A question with both outcomes, the one that keeps the answer first
+    # (Failure 100), and no "you answered X, but" (Failure 98).
+    cited = {c.clause_id for c in citations}
+    timed = {c.clause_id for c in computed.waiting}
+    if verdict == Verdict.NOT_COVERED and cited and cited <= computed.exclusions - timed:
+        looked_up = {
+            clause_id: (f"excludes \"{words}\"", "an arrest or an offence",
+                        "What is being treated is an illness, or an injury from something "
+                        "else, or the person committed no offence",
+                        "The person was hurt while committing or attempting an offence "
+                        "with criminal intent, or as a direct result of it")
+            for clause_id, words in computed.breach_of_law.items()
+        } | {
+            clause_id: ("is an EXCLUSION about alcohol, intoxicating substances or drugs",
+                        "drink or drugs",
+                        "What is being treated was not caused by drink or drugs",
+                        "What is being treated is attributable to what that clause excludes")
+            for clause_id in computed.intoxication
+        }
+        for clause_id, (says, mentions, unrelated, applies_when) in looked_up.items():
+            if clause_id in cited:
+                continue
+            problems.append(
+                f"- Clause {clause_id} {says}. The person mentions {mentions} "
+                f"(looked up from their own words; a lookup cannot tell whether "
+                f"that has anything to do with this treatment), and the previous "
+                f"answer refused this claim without citing clause {clause_id}. "
+                f"Decide which of these the person described. (a) {unrelated}: "
+                f"clause {clause_id} does not apply and is not cited, and the "
+                f"answer rests on the other clauses. (b) {applies_when}: clause "
+                f"{clause_id} is the clause that refuses this claim, cited with "
+                f"effect denies, and another clause is cited as well only if it "
+                f"refuses THIS claim on what the person actually described. Say "
+                f"which in the reasoning."
+            )
